  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThrough Monday...Windy this afternoon with gusts to 35 mph at\ntimes. Diminishing pressure gradient and weakening low-level jet\ntonight will allow for lighter winds late tonight into Monday. As\nwinds become light, could be a touch of fog late tonight central\nto north-central WI. Temperatures this afternoon and Monday\nafternoon will reach the lower 60s. Average highs for mid October\nare in the mid to upper 50s.  \n\nDry and warm this week, with small chances for rain next weekend...\nRising mid-level heights with prevailing high pressure at surface\nadd up to mainly dry conditions this week. Still a very small \n(10-20%) chance of showers showing up on LREF late Wednesday into \nWednesday night, but main lift/moisture remains north of Lake \nSuperior. NBM pops remain less than 10%. Approaching front with \nmain sfc low over central Canada is showing up as low chances \n(20-40%) for showers Saturday night into Sunday. Moisture is \nlacking with the front though, so this does not look to produce \nmuch rain if it even occurs. This shows up when looking at \nprobabilities of seeing over 0.10 inch, which are less than 10%. \nTemperatures in the lower 60s early this week will warm steadily\ninto the upper 60s to even lower 70s by late this week into next\nweekend depending on cloud cover, rain chances. \n\nLate night, morning fog this week...High pressure drifting across\nand nighttime cooling, light winds results in setup for at least \npatchy ground fog especially Monday night through Wednesday night.\nA couple of these nights could have locally dense fog. \n\nLooking at the longer range, there is no signal at this point of\nany significant pattern change per the ECMWF into early November \nand the time lagged CFS data into mid November. This lines up with\nlatest 3-4 week outlook from CPC. Thus, warmer and dry weather will\nbe the theme until further notice. With that, we'll have to keep eye\non fire weather concerns, especially on windier days.",
